								&nbsp;<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php#1">General Category</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?board=1.0">General Discussion</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1281.0#msg12756">Re: Is it really all down hill from here?</a>
							</td>
							<td align="right" style="padding: 0 1ex; white-space: nowrap;">
								on: November 19, 2004, 08:31:02 am
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">While it is unlikely that there will be &quot;one big EQ killer&quot; (EQ2 has the biggest chance lately since it is marketed directly to current EQ subscribers) there will be a constant chipping away at the playerbase of EQ by every game that comes out. Each new MMOG that comes along will offer some carrot that EQL does not offer. The playerbase will shrink, but the game will not die. It is arguably a better platform than UO, and that one is still around despite rampant cheating and an inferior engine.<br /><br />It is however likely that there will be several phases of &quot;growing pains&quot; as the playerbase shrinks, and it becomes more and more difficult to keep a raiding core together. <br /><br />Guilds are maintained by a couple of quantities: <br />1) The number of &quot;hardcore&quot; players on the server. Hardcore is described as people who play alot, and are likely &quot;but not required&quot; to be uber. We all know that guilds are run a small group of dedicated people. The greater the number of hardcore players, the more guilds can be maintained on the server. <br />2) Content limits the number of guilds as well. The less the content, the fewer guilds can be supported on the server. This was a bigger issue in the old days, now there is so much content, guilds skip large chunks of it. (yes i know it is a function of risk/reward but in theory the server has more content than guilds can utilize) <br />3) Sheer number of people. There is a critical mass of people required for a guild to progress anywhere in EQ. This number has changed over the years as content has been added, but once a guild drops too many &quot;warm bodies&quot; regardless of talent, or dedication, the guild is essentially doomed.<br /><br />When the server population drops, requirement 3 drops significantly, and guilds loose the &quot;warm body&quot; factor. What has to happen is a reduction of the number of guilds to regain that critical mass of bodies once again. We will see a severe reduction in the number of top quality guilds, and I would suspect that the requrements to be in the top 10 will actually be lowered, as there will be actually fewer guilds to choose from.<br /><br />At some point Sony will find that they have to merge the servers to save money, and because there simply wont be enough people on each server to support many guilds. EQ will continue to exist, as long as it can make money, and merging servers will allow it to do so. EQ will fade, it will not die.</td>
